Reduced Docker Image Size from 1.2GB → 180MB (Real Optimization Story)

---

🐳 Our Docker image was 1.2GB and deployments were taking too long.
Build time slow.
Push/Pull slow.
Startup slow.

This was costing us time AND money.

Here’s exactly how I optimized it 👇

---

Root Cause

We were using a single-stage Dockerfile:

```
FROM openjdk:11
COPY . .
RUN mvn clean install
CMD ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]
```

Big base image + all build dependencies + final jar = HUGE image.

---

🧠 Solution — Multi-Stage Build

✔ Stage 1: Build the app

```
FROM maven:3.8 AS build
WORKDIR /app
COPY . .
RUN mvn clean install
```

 ✔ Stage 2: Final runtime

```
FROM openjdk:11-jre
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /app/target/app.jar app.jar
CMD ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]
```

---

🚀 Result

✔ Image size reduced from 1.2GB → 180MB
✔ Faster build & deploy
✔ Faster pod startup
✔ Less traffic on registry
✔ Lower storage cost

---

💡 Why It Works

Multi-stage build only copies:

* final artifact (JAR)
* not build tools
* not source code
* not temporary files

So final image is lean & production-ready.

---

🔧 Bonus Tip

Use Alpine base image when possible:

```
FROM eclipse-temurin:17-jre-alpine
```

Image size becomes even smaller.
But test first — Alpine may break native libs.

🌟 Key Takeaways

* Don’t push huge images to production
* Multi-stage builds are MUST for DevOps
* Your CI/CD pipeline becomes faster
* Kubernetes startup becomes faster
* You save cost automatically

Small optimization → huge impact.
